{#
    The top navigation: where a reader can go from anywhere.

    Configuration, like the mark and the footer — a theme that read the
    toctree here would put every page of a manual in the bar, which is what
    the rail is for. What belongs up here is the handful of places a site has,
    and only the site knows them:

        <navigation>
            <link href="/frontend" label="Frontend"/>
            <link href="https://example.org" label="Elsewhere" external="true"/>
        </navigation>

    `sds-menu` and not a bare row of pills: what happens to four words when the
    header runs out of room is the element's business, measured rather than
    guessed at a breakpoint. The links are written here because this side knows
    where they point and which one the reader is in; the element moves them
    into a panel below the bar when they stop fitting.

    A section is current when the reader is on it *or* under it — the rootline
    is the toctree walked upwards, so a guideline page marks Guidelines. Marking
    only the exact page left the bar saying nothing on every page but five.

    The active pill is a filled block rather than a tint, and it is one of
    exactly three places the accent appears.
#}
{%- if soul.navigation.links is defined and soul.navigation.links %}
    <sds-menu label="Sections">
        {%- for link in soul.navigation.links %}
            {%- set here = not link.external and link.href|trim('/') in env.currentFileRootline %}
            <a class="sds-pill{% if here %} is-active{% endif %}"
               href="{{ link.external ? link.href : renderLink(link.href) }}"
               {%- if link.external %} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"{% endif %}
               {%- if here %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>{{ link.label }}</a>
        {%- endfor %}
    </sds-menu>
{%- endif -%}
